WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:06.009
From underseen comedies and totally insane
gorefests to introspective dramas that have

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:11.880
gotten lost in the shuffle, 2021 has contained
a heap of cinematic surprises that all deserve

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.590
to get their time in the spotlight.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.710
Here are the most underrated movies of 2021
so far.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.881
Have you ever sat down and asked yourself,
"What if there was a Power Rangers movie that

00:00:21.881 --> 00:00:25.840
also contained grotesque gore and horror but
was also super self-referential?"

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.180
If so, then that very specific film you're
looking for is finally here!

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:36.260
"PG: Psycho Goreman" is the late-night alien
comedy you never knew you needed.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:41.649
It's a ridiculously cheesy and self-aware
story of a bloodthirsty alien warrior brought

00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:45.780
back to life who must do the bidding of a
little girl, as she possesses the gem that

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.300
holds all his power.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:53.069
An eye-popping, comedically anarchic film,
"PG: Psycho Goreman" is that perfect midnight

00:00:53.069 --> 00:00:55.160
pulp film to catch with your friends.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.269
This bizarre Canadian flick is filled with
gross-out horror, ridiculously silly humor,

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:03.199
and enough eye-catching creature effects to
keep you entertained.

00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:07.409
Plus, there's a heap of silly performances
and some truly stellar costume and puppet

00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:08.409
work.

00:01:08.409 --> 00:01:12.649
Let's hope that maybe we'll get "PG: Psycho
Goreman 2 – The Return of Alistair the Brain

00:01:12.649 --> 00:01:13.649
Bot."

00:01:13.649 --> 00:01:16.060
"Well, it was nice meeting you."

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:19.620
"It would be nicer if you were dead."

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.620
"Alright, bye."

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.710
With its initial release coming right in the
middle of Oscar season, where attention was

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:30.460
being lavished on a heap of other films, it's
only fair to shine a light on "The White Tiger."

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:34.990
This Netflix film was able to snag a Best
Adapted Screenplay nomination at the 93rd

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:35.990
Academy Awards.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.140
However, other than that very notable Oscar
nod, this movie has been flying under the

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.140
radar.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.030
However, as it's been compared to beloved
films like "Joker" and "Parasite," it's a

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.090
film that definitely deserves some attention.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:51.820
Adapted and directed by critically acclaimed
director Ramin Bahrani, "The White Tiger"

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.990
follows the young Balram as he tries to navigate
the brutal class system in India.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.140
However, his lower-class beginnings shape
the world around him as he tries to climb

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.259
the capitalist ladder, a ladder that can only
lead to the worst places.

00:02:04.259 --> 00:02:09.440
With an exacting eye, lush visuals, and brilliant
social commentary, "The White Tiger" is a

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:13.580
film worth seeking out amongst Netflix's wide
array of feature film options.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.410
Are we destined to be miserable?

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.330
Is marriage really supposed to get worse as
the years go on?

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:23.300
If you're still happy in your marriage 14
years in, is there something wrong with you?

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.250
Like, actually wrong with you?

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.470
Those are the questions brought up in the
intriguingly strange "Happily," a comedy that

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:33.860
seems to have slipped under the radar in these
weird pandemic times, but it still delivers

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.480
on its off-kilter premise with surprising
results.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.470
Joel McHale and Kerry Bishe star as the central
couple of this story, who receive a visit

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.130
from a mysterious stranger who explains that
their wonderfully happy marriage isn't all

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.480
it seems to be.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:52.440
What follows from there would be too fascinating
to spoil, but it all leads up to a fancy getaway

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.570
that isn’t all that it seems.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:59.530
It's a strange and ultimately daring comedy
that asks the sad questions of life that we've

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.660
always been too afraid to ask ourselves.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:07.090
There have been plenty of documentaries over
the years following the lives of the rich

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:11.200
and famous, especially musicians and recording
artists we know and love.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:15.780
These films can range from incisive, introspective
looks inside the world of an artist or just

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.320
another piece of puff-filled P.R. to boost
an artist's ego.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:24.000
"Billie Eilish: The World's a Little Blurry,"
thankfully, sits primarily in the former category,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.860
providing audiences a dark and detailed look
inside the world of this particular brilliant

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:29.860
performer.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:33.570
We see the emotional, mental, and physical
strain it takes to become one of the world's

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:34.630
leading pop stars.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:38.700
"The World's a Little Blurry" follows the
entire songwriting process of Eilish's hit

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.680
debut album all the way to her titanic Grammy
Awards sweep.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.600
Eilish comes across as a wonderfully personable
figure with little desire for the glam of

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:51.790
celebrity and a hyper-focus on the passion
of her songwriting and creative output.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:57.000
It's an epic and humanizing film, one that
gives us that most wondrous and personal look

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.900
at a world that can often seem so far away.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.970
Is "Shiva Baby" a comedy or a horror film?

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:07.920
More likely, it's some brilliant, fantastically
Jewish amalgam of the two.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.790
It’s a combination that's only possible
through the mind of writer-director Emma Seligman

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:15.210
in this frighteningly funny debut feature.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:19.549
Rachel Sennott stars as Danielle, a college
senior who's heading straight from her appointment

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:22.940
with her sugar daddy to meet up with her family
at a shiva.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.350
But when these two worlds suddenly combine,
the levels of anxiety and awkwardness build

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.400
in a most nightmarish fashion.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.230
The film features some absolutely fierce and
passionate scenes of reckoning with growing

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:38.330
up in contemporary Jewish America, all packed
into a tight 77-minute runtime.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:43.090
In other words, "Shiva Baby" is the perfect
comedy to remind you of the horrors and anxieties

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:46.810
of the world, and it'll give you a good excuse
to laugh and cry about them all day.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:52.070
When all is said and done, we'll all look
back at 2021 as the year when one of the most

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:56.330
ridiculously joyful comedies ever made snuck
into the world.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:05:01.370
"Barb &amp; Star Go to Vista Del Mar” is Kristin
Wiig and Annie Mumolo's long-awaited follow-up

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.870
to their Oscar-nominated hit "Bridesmaids."

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:08.760
It takes that same kernel of the power of
female friendship and explodes it into a madcap

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:14.530
adventure following two middle-aged women
taking the grand vacation they've always wanted.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:19.260
“Let’s go to Vista Del Mar!”

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.990
Where "Bridesmaids" reveled in its down-to-earth
improv-style comedic trappings, "Barb &amp; Star"

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.740
feels like a Muppet movie where all the Muppets
have been replaced by humans.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:32.680
It's a bright and shining absurdist piece
of insanity, with talking animals, evil villains,

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.780
and Jamie Dornan singing a tragic love song
to a flock of seagulls.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:40.900
It's a film that will absolutely make you
grin from ear to ear, and if nothing else,

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.060
you'll never look at culottes the same way
again.

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:48.200
The most important thing to know is that Derek
DelGaudio wants you to leave your assumptions

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.460
about him at the door.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:54.150
That's all part of the illusion — both literally
and spiritually — embedded in "In &amp; Of Itself."

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:58.810
It’s a filmed version of DelGaudio's hit
stage show that has confounded and enlightened

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:02.930
off-Broadway audiences under the direction
of the legendary Frank Oz.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:07.490
Now available to stream on Hulu, DelGaudio's
solo performance and illusionary spectacle

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:10.120
is honestly better the less you know going
in.

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:15.340
Even so, be ready for a wonderfully introspective
piece of theatrical filmmaking that dares

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:19.470
to ask us all if we really know who we are
at the end of the day.

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:23.370
At the beginning of the film, the text on
the screen asks us to put our phones away,

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:27.650
and it's advice best heeded for this wonderfully
underrated piece of magical filmmaking at

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:28.650
its finest.

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.680
If you're someone who's all in on the world
of comedic horror, then you definitely need

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.180
to know about Josh Ruben.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:39.990
He displayed a knack for delivering expert
spooky laughs in 2020's "Scare Me" which he

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.800
directed as well as starred in.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:46.300
Now, Ruben stays behind the camera for this
year's nightmarishly funny "Werewolves Within."

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:51.210
It’s a movie that's also, inexplicably,
one of the most entertaining video game-to-film

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.620
adaptations in recent memory.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.860
Based on the 2016 Mafia-style game of the
same name, "Werewolves Within" takes place

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:00.010
in the small town of Beaverfield.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.550
It follows the one ranger who's been tasked
with finding the hairy monster that hides

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.730
within this less-than-assuming village.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:10.540
Anchored by a lead performance from the always
wonderful Sam Richardson, "Werewolves Within"

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:14.220
has more than enough laughs, frights, and
genuine craft behind the camera.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.740
It’s definitely worthy of being made a new
staple of the Halloween season, and we can't

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.610
wait to see what Ruben cooks up next.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:26.470
Edgar Wright makes his freshman foray into
the world of documentary film with "The Sparks

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:27.470
Brothers."

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.870
Whether you're a lifelong fan of the notorious
music group Sparks — comprised of real-life

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:35.730
brothers Ron and Russell Mael or just hearing
of them, this is just the thing for you.

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:40.169
It chronicles the duo's musical journey over
50 years and features interviews with titans

00:07:40.169 --> 00:07:42.050
of the entertainment industry.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:46.870
Wright's signature frenetic and free-wheeling
style of filmmaking is used to glorious effect

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:50.900
here to make you fall in love with this dynamic
duo as fast as Wright did.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:55.180
This is a huge year for the music duo, as
they're also the songwriters and creative

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:58.729
voices behind the new Leos Carax movie musical
"Annette."

00:07:58.729 --> 00:08:03.080
Who knew that 2021 would be the year that
these genius creative voices would emerge

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:06.060
into the mainstream to encompass the world
of entertainment?

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.450
Maybe it's just the way the Sparks brothers
wanted it all along.

00:08:09.639 --> 00:08:13.889
It's unclear how we got to this incredible
point where Steven Soderbergh is able to churn

00:08:13.889 --> 00:08:18.229
out at least one film a year, but why argue
with something so wonderful?

00:08:18.229 --> 00:08:23.260
Ever since his "un-retirement" in 2017 with
the exquisitely goofy "Logan Lucky," Soderbergh

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.590
has been steadily releasing new films.

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:30.229
He's been taking each film as a new chance
to experiment with form, craft, and storytelling,

00:08:30.229 --> 00:08:34.860
resulting in — if nothing else — fascinating
new cinematic entries on a yearly basis.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.599
And this year's film, "No Sudden Move," is
nothing short of one of Soderbergh's most

00:08:38.599 --> 00:08:41.349
entertaining and exciting movies to date.

00:08:41.349 --> 00:08:46.509
Set in 1950s Detroit, Soderbergh's latest
zippy heist follows three gangsters brought

00:08:46.509 --> 00:08:50.899
onto a blackmail heist, tasked with holding
hostage the family of an accountant, played

00:08:50.899 --> 00:08:52.110
by David Harbour.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:56.800
With enough twists, turns, and overtly passionate
screeds about the dangers of big business

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:57.800
and capitalism.

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:01.689
"No Sudden Move" proves that when it comes
to the heist film, Soderbergh isn't getting

00:09:01.689 --> 00:09:03.839
rusty anytime soon.

